---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import JpgToPng from '@/components/tools/image-convert/JpgToPng.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="jpg-to-png">
  <JpgToPng client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert JPG to PNG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your JPG or JPEG files onto the box above. You can add any number of files. The tool converts them one at a time with a progress bar.</li>
      <li>Leave the transparency option off, because a JPEG has no transparent pixels. Switch it on only if you want a solid colour painted again behind the image.</li>
      <li>Save each PNG, or download all of them as a single ZIP.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This page has no quality slider, because PNG is lossless. The encoder writes exactly the pixels that came
      out of the JPEG decoder.
    </p>

    <h2>JPG to PNG does not repair a JPEG</h2>
    <p>
      People often convert JPG to PNG to improve quality. It cannot. JPEG compression is destructive and
      happens once, when the file is first written. The discarded detail is gone. PNG keeps what remains,
      including each block artefact and each halo around text.
    </p>
    <p>
      But PNG does not lose more quality. If you will crop, annotate and save an image several times, do that
      work in PNG, so each save does not add another generation of loss.
    </p>

    <h2>Expect the file to get much bigger</h2>
    <p>
      Lossless compression must describe each pixel, and photographs have millions of slightly different colours
      that no dictionary compressor can collapse. Rough figures for the same source image:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left">Image type</th>
            <th align="left">As JPEG (q85)</th>
            <th align="left">As PNG</th>
            <th align="left">Ratio</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>12 MP phone photo</td><td>~2.5 MB</td><td>~18 MB</td><td>7×</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1920 × 1080 landscape</td><td>~380 KB</td><td>~3.2 MB</td><td>8×</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flat illustration, few colours</td><td>~140 KB</td><td>~90 KB</td><td>0.6×</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scanned text page</td><td>~250 KB</td><td>~180 KB</td><td>0.7×</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In the last two rows, PNG is smaller. For images with large areas of identical colour, converting a JPEG
      to PNG makes the file smaller and cleaner.
    </p>

    <h2>Good reasons to convert</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A tool accepts only PNG. Many icon pipelines, game engines and print templates refuse JPEG.</li>
      <li>You need an alpha channel. Convert first, then cut out a background and keep it transparent.</li>
      <li>You need a lossless intermediate for editing. Convert once, make all your edits, and export to JPEG at the end.</li>
      <li>You want to remove metadata. Re-encoding drops all EXIF, which removes GPS coordinates, the camera's serial number and the capture timestamp from a photo before you share it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Photos taken in portrait orientation have an EXIF rotation flag, not rotated pixels. This converter
      applies that flag during decoding, so your PNG is the right way up. For a smaller file that is not
      lossless, try
      <a href="/jpg-to-webp">JPG to WebP</a> or the universal <a href="/image-converter">image converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
